# G029 — Random Gift Suggestions

*v2. See `gift-suggestions.md` for prose: first recommendation engine,
fuzzy matching via relevance scoring (leap from search to recommend),
traits as profiles, budget as filter composed over ranking.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Agent Discovery via Capability Profiles

[agent-discovery @requester @required-traits @budget
    IN ORDER TO
        find the best-fit agent whose capability profile covers the task
        within the requester's resource budget — rank then filter
        because best-within-constraint is the universal selection shape

    @catalog <- [registry[all-agents]]

    @ranked <- @catalog mapped to
        {agent: @a,
         score: [match[score
                         {profile: @a:capabilities,
                          traits:  @required-traits}]]}
        ordered by score descending

    @affordable <- @ranked
        where @a:hourly-cost is at most @budget
        first

    @affordable
        where exists
            -> [dispatch {to: @affordable:agent,
                           from: @requester}]
        otherwise
            -> #no-agent-within-budget
]

---

## Verb Surface

[match
    score              {profile, traits}        ;; -> Int (count of matches)
]

[gifts
    catalog
    suggest            {catalog, recipient-traits, budget}
    suggest-top-n      {catalog, recipient-traits, budget, n}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: score then filter `where` rank leaves budget for later

[gifts[suggest {catalog: @c, recipient-traits: @t, budget: @b}]
    IN ORDER TO
        score every gift by trait overlap first
        then filter surviving gifts by budget
        so the ranking is independent of the constraint — two operations
        compose without either knowing the other

    @c:gifts
        mapped to {gift: @g,
                    score: count (@g:traits intersected-with @t)}
        ordered by score descending, then price ascending
        where price is at most @b
        first                       ;; take top
]

Selection is boolean; ranking is continuous. The leap from
"does it match?" to "how well does it match?" is the leap from
search to recommendation — G029 introduces the distinction.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: profile matching IS agent discovery in disguise

Gift catalog = agent registry.
Recipient traits = task requirements.
Relevance score = fit metric.
Budget = resource constraint.
`suggest` = `discover-agent`.

The choreography is identical. Only the domain vocabulary changes.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @c <- [gifts[catalog]]

    @traits <- ["book-lover", "quiet", "analytical"]
    -> [gifts[suggest-top-n
                {catalog: @c,
                 recipient-traits: @traits,
                 budget: 40,
                 n: 3}]]
    ;; -> three gifts, highest trait-overlap first, each ≤ $40
]
